    {topic:"",q:"The forces which govern the coning angle are.",opts:["lift and centrifugal force.","thrust and centrifugal force.","lift and thrust."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"As a helicopter accelerates in level forward flight above approximately 15 knots, it will be necessary to:.",opts:["reduce power because of the additional lift due to translational flight.","increase power because rotor RPM is increasing.","reduce power because rotor profile drag is reduced."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Assuming the phase lag of a rotor blade is 90o and the control advance angle is 15 degrees, then the pitch operating arm must be at the highest point of the swash plate:.",opts:["90o ahead of the highest flapping position.","75o ahead of the highest flapping position.","105o ahead of the highest flapping position."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A helicopter is hovering and the pilot applies right pedal.Assuming the main rotor rotates anti clockwise viewed from above, the helicopter will.",opts:["descend, unless the pilot inches the throttle open.","ascend, unless the pilot decreases rotor RPM.","descend, unless the pilot applies more collective pitch."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A helicopter has a main rotor which rotates anti-clockwise viewed from above, and is fitted with an anti-torque tail rotor. It will tend to drift sideways to.",opts:["port, if the tail rotor is mounted on the left side of the aircraft.","starboard, whichever way the tail rotor is fitted.","port, if the tail rotor is mounted on the right side of the aircraft."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"As the rotor head is tilted to travel forward, what happens to the rearward travelling blade's pitch angle?.",opts:["Increases.","Decreases.","No change."],ans:0,exp:"A&P Technician Airframe Textbook Pg.1-59."},
    {topic:"",q:"The tail rotor.",opts:["produces a force opposing torque reaction.","produces a force in the same direction as torque reaction.","is not subject to dissymmetry of lift."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"With the tail rotor pedals in neutral, the tail rotor blade pitch will be.",opts:["positive.","negative.","neutral."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The main rotor drive shaft is tilted laterally on some helicopters to correct tail rotor.",opts:["drift.","torque.","roll."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Certain helicopters tend to tilt laterally when landing. This problem can be overcome by placing the tail rotor thrust.",opts:["below the line of the main rotor hub.","above the line of the main rotor hub.","in line with the main rotor hub."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Ground cushion effect is produced by.",opts:["recirculating air giving additional lift.","increased pressure under the main rotor disc.","increase in density above the fuselage."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A helicopter hovering near a tall building will.",opts:["drift away from it.","be unaffected by it.","drift towards it."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Drooping of helicopter blades is compensated by.",opts:["flapping.","dragging.","centrifugal force."],ans:2,exp:"Centrifugal force counteracts the droop of helicopter blades at low RPM."},
    {topic:"",q:"During decent with power-on, on a helicopter.",opts:["lift, weight and thrust are acting on the helicopter.","lift, drag and thrust are acting on the helicopter.","lift, drag, thrust and weight are acting on the helicopter."],ans:2,exp:"All four forces act on the helicopter."},
    {topic:"",q:"A helicopter hovering 2m above the ground subject to a strong cross wind will.",opts:["lose lift due to the removal of the ground cushion effect.","increase lift due to ground cushion effect.","lose lift due to recirculation."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"With a drop in ambient temperature, an aircraft service ceiling will.",opts:["lower.","rise.","not be affected."],ans:1,exp:"As ambient temperature drops, density increases and aircraft performance increases."},
    {topic:"",q:"During an autorotative descent, rotor RPM will be.",opts:["higher than in powered flight.","lower than in powered flight.","substantially the same as in powered flight."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Helicopter blades are.",opts:["symmetrical.","highly cambered.","reverse cambered."],ans:0,exp:"Helicopter blades are usually symmetrical section."},
    {topic:"",q:"Autorotative force is the.",opts:["component of the total reaction which acts forward in the plane of rotation in opposition to drag.","force required to turn in a hover.","force the pilot must apply to the collective lever to obtain a controlled descent."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Autorotation.",opts:["leaves the aircraft with no directional control.","results in a loss of power.","is the production of lift from freely rotating rotor blades."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the main rotor of a helicopter rotates in an anti-clockwise direction when viewed from above, and a hovering left turn is required, the following movements of the controls are selected:.",opts:["Rudder pedal to the left, and decrease throttle.","Rudder pedal to the left, and increase throttle.","Rudder pedal to the right, and increase throttle."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Helicopter derives its lift from.",opts:["air is pushed downward.","rotor acts as a airscrew.","the blade of the helicopter creates a low pressure above it."],ans:2,exp:"All three are correct, but an aerodynamicist would choose a 'creates a low pressure above it'."},
    {topic:"",q:"A two bladed helicopter rotor on a central gimbal is called.",opts:["semi rigid rotor.","fully articulated rotor.","rigid rotor."],ans:0,exp:"A&P Technician Airframe Textbook Pg.1-53."},
    {topic:"",q:"If a helicopter rotor disc is rotating anticlockwise, viewed from above where, would a pitch input be fed into the disc to move the helicopter backwards, (90 degrees to what)?.",opts:["In front of the lateral axis.","Left of the longitudinal axis.","Right of the longitudinal axis."],ans:2,exp:"Due to precession of the rotor disc, the input to tilt the disc backwards must be placed 90 degrees before, in direction of motion of the disc."},
    {topic:"",q:"On a helicopter, what is vortex ring state?.",opts:["Tip vortex build-up during hover.","Tip vortex interference at high forward speed.","Ground vortex interference when hovering close to the ground."],ans:0,exp:"During hover, the tip vortex of one blade adds to the tip vortices of the preceding blades, producing a large vortex ring and a very inefficient helicopter."},
    {topic:"",q:"Climbs with forward speed require less power than vertical climbs, because of.",opts:["translational lift.","increased inertia.","forward momentum."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Translational flight is.",opts:["achieved by raising or lowering the collective lever.","when the helicopter changes from one steady flight condition to another.","achieved by tilting the rotor disc in the direction of flight."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The best design of a rotor blade is where the CofP.",opts:["moves freely along the length of the blade.","does not move.","is insignificant."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When the cyclic stick is eased forward in the hover position.",opts:["altitude is increased.","forward thrust is decreased.","vertical lift is reduced."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The rotor cone is formed by.",opts:["blade alignment.","centrifugal force and lift.","centrifugal force only."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Tracking is carried out to_____________the main rotor blade tip path.",opts:["restore.","align.","balance."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The advancing blade of a helicopter is the one moving.",opts:["one moving forward into relative airflow.","highest blade.","one moving in direction of relative air flow."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Lift is generated by.",opts:["down-wash below the blade.","high pressure above the blade.","low pressure above the blade."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Static stability of a helicopter is.",opts:["the stability of the helicopter when hovering.","the tendency move back toward neutral after disturbance.","the tendency to oscillate until the neutral is achieved."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Forward velocity causes the advancing blade to.",opts:["flap down to increase lift.","give increased lift due to blade flapping.","flap up to reduce lift."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When moving from the hover to forward flight it is necessary to.",opts:["increase the engine power.","decrease the engine power.","maintain constant engine power."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"After a change in collective pitch the Rotor RPM will rise and fall. This is called.",opts:["static droop.","transient droop.","under swing."],ans:1,exp:"The Helicopter and How it Flies by John Fay Page 20/21. The initial total fall in rotor RPM is called transient droop. The change in stabilised RPM is referred to as static droop. The RPM difference between transient droop and static droop is called the underswing."},
    {topic:"",q:"After a change in pitch of a rotor blade, the blade will be at maximum flap at.",opts:["90°.","0°.","180°."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The rotor disc is.",opts:["the ground cushion.","the distance between tip to tip.","the rotor head hub."],ans:1,exp:"Automatic Flight Control. Pallett. Page 59/60 fig 1.42."},
    {topic:"",q:"The maximum forward speed of a helicopter is limited by.",opts:["retreating blade stall and the forward speed of the advancing blade.","engine power.","the shape of the fuselage."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What principle does the delta 3 hinge use?.",opts:["Triangular pitch change lever.","Flapping actuators.","Offset hinges."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"As the angle of attack of a rotor blade increases, it affects the.",opts:["flapping forces.","dragging and the flapping forces.","dragging forces."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"With an increase in its angle of attack, the drag acting on a rotor blade.",opts:["decreases.","increases.","remains constant."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Forces on a helicopter, in a power-on descent are.",opts:["lift, drag, thrust, weight.","lift, drag, thrust.","weight, drag, lift."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The bell stability augmentation system is based on.",opts:["flapping hinges.","offset hinges.","gyroscopic forces."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"During forward flight the advancing blade will.",opts:["flap down.","flap up.","lag."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the advancing blade on a helicopter doing?.",opts:["Going to the highest point.","Increasing in lift.","Increasing in drag."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What is the swash plate on a helicopter used for?.",opts:["Control of the pitch of the rotor blades.","Control of the speed of the rotor blades.","Control of the flap of the rotor blades."],ans:0,exp:"Automatic Flight Control Pallett Page 61."},
    {topic:"",q:"Upwash on a helicopter would result in.",opts:["decrease in lift.","increase in lift without an increase in power.","decrease in speed."],ans:1,exp:"Upwash increases angle of attack."},
    {topic:"",q:"After a roll to the left of a statically unstable helicopter, the helicopter would.",opts:["continue to roll further.","remain at the position that it had rolled to.","roll back to the horizontal."],ans:0,exp:"Automatic Flight Control Pallett Page 65."},
    {topic:"",q:"The difference between transient droop and static droop is.",opts:["overswing.","underswing.","a hole in one."],ans:1,exp:"The Helicopter history, piloting and how it flies John Fay ISBN81-70002-030- 1 Page No 21\"."},
    {topic:"",q:"What happens to a helicopter in autorotative flight?.",opts:["The rotor goes in the normal direction of rotation.","The rotor goes the opposite direction to the normal direction of rotation.","The cabin goes in the direction of rotation."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The RPM of the rotor blades is constant, within small limits to.",opts:["prevent blades over-speeding.","prevent blades folding up during flight.","reduce torque loading."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"To maintain the position of the helicopter, when hovering with a decrease in air density, the pilot must.",opts:["increase the collective pitch.","increase rotor RPM.","increase the cyclic pitch."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Relative airflow over a helicopter blade.",opts:["increases at the tip.","is unaffected by blade position.","increases at the root."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An helicopter fin helps to give.",opts:["longitudinal stability about the normal axis.","directional stability about the normal axis.","directional stability about the longitudinal axis."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"After a roll to the left of a statically stable helicopter, the helicopter would.",opts:["continue to roll.","come back to level flight.","increases roll."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Solidity of the rotor is the ratio of the.",opts:["blade area to disc area.","all up weight to blade area.","all up weight to disc area."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Which direction is the air flowing through the main rotor during autorotation?.",opts:["Upwards.","Parallel to the rotor chord line.","Downwards."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The purpose of an off-set vertical stabilizer is to.",opts:["provide stability during vertical flight.","relieve some of the load on the tail rotor during forward flight.","provide lift during forward flight."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The purpose of the horizontal stabilizer is to.",opts:["maintain the aircraft in as near a horizontal attitude as possible, during forward flight.","to reduce rotor head loads during translational flight.","to stabilize the aircraft in the hover."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"During autorotation, the rudder pedals.",opts:["would need to be backed off due to the loss of torque.","would have no effect on directional control.","would need to be advanced to counteract the increased torque."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In forward flight, the advancing blade would be expected to.",opts:["lag.","increase pitch.","flap up."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When the helicopter moves from the hover to translational flight, the lift vector will.",opts:["remain vertical.","move forward.","move aft."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the blade angle of attack increases.",opts:["lift increases only.","lift and drag increases.","drag increases only."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When a blade is flapping up.",opts:["pitch will increase.","pitch will decrease.","lift and drag both increase."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Vortex ring start requires.",opts:["retreating blade stall.","advancing blade stall.","power on descent."],ans:2,exp:"The helicopter and how it flies Page 117 by John Fay."},
    {topic:"",q:"Rotor tip vortices are strongest when.",opts:["hovering with high weight.","flying high speed in straight and level flight.","flying into a headwind."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Tail rotor effects the helicopter in.",opts:["pitch and roll.","vertical axis.","horizontal axis."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A blade of a Helicopter Main Rotor is.",opts:["highly cambered.","reverse cambered.","symmetrically cambered."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Helicopter rotor blades produce lift by.",opts:["moving a small mass of air downwards slowly.","moving a large mass of air downwards quickly.","creating a lower pressure above the blade than below."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"With an increase in forward velocity of a helicopter, the increase in parasitic drag will cause the fuselage attitude to.",opts:["pitch down.","remain level.","pitch up."],ans:0,exp:"The amount of parasitic drag increases with increasing airspeed, so that the thrust/parasite drag (nose down) couple becomes stronger."},
    {topic:"",q:"On a helicopter, rotor disc lift happens.",opts:["180° later.","90° later.","immediately."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"During an autorotative decent, rotor RPM will be.",opts:["higher than in powered flight.","approximately the same as in powered flight.","lower than in powered flight."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Lift in a helicopter is a result of.",opts:["pitch * square root of speed.","angle of attack * velocity squared.","angle of attack * velocity squared and forward speed."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"After a change in pitch of a rotor blade the blade will be at maximum flap at.",opts:["180°.","90°.","0°."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When the rotor blade increases its angle of attack, the centre of pressure.",opts:["does not move.","moves rearwards.","moves forward."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Autorotative force attempts to pull the rotor blade.",opts:["in the direction of normal rotation.","both in and against the direction of normal rotation.","against the direction of normal rotation."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The ground effect is effective up to a height equalling.",opts:["twice the diameter of the rotor disc.","the length of the fuselage.","the diameter of the rotor disc."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Movement of the collective control will.",opts:["increase the pitch of the main rotor blades.","increase the pitch of the tail rotor.","tilt the disc and increase engine power."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Rotor blade sailing is a problem at.",opts:["low rotor RPM at engine shut down.","normal rotor RPM in gusty conditions.","high rotor RPM at engine start up."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The layer of air over the surface of an aerofoil which is slower moving, in relation to the rest of the airflow, is known as.",opts:["camber layer.","boundary layer.","none of the above are correct."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"During helicopter forward flight the retreating blade will tend to.",opts:["flap down.","no change.","flap up."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When airflow velocity over an upper cambered surface of an aerofoil decreases, what takes place?.",opts:["Pressure increases, lift decreases.","Pressure decreases, lift increases.","Pressure increases, lift increases."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What is a controlling factor of turbulence and skin friction?.",opts:["Countersunk rivets used on skin exterior.","Fineness ratio.","Aspect ratio."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Changes in aircraft weight.",opts:["will only affect total drag if the lift is kept constant.","will not affect total drag since it is dependant only upon speed.","cause corresponding changes in total drag due to the associated lift change."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When an aerofoil stalls.",opts:["lift and drag increase.","lift decreases and drag increases.","lift increases and drag decreases."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When a helicopter rotor disc is tilted forward, what happens to the pitch of the retreating blade?.",opts:["Increases.","Remains constant.","Decreases."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Airflow through the main rotor disc in autorotation is.",opts:["always down through the rotor disc.","always up through the rotor disc.","may be either up or down."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The blade stalling speed will.",opts:["only change if the MTWA were changed.","increase with an increase in helicopter weight.","be unaffected by helicopter weight changes since it is dependant upon the angle of attack."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Ground effect will be most apparent when.",opts:["taxiing in the hover.","hovering close to the ground.","taxiing on the ground."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When entering into a stable autorotative state, the main rotor RPM will initially.",opts:["increase.","decrease.","be unaffected."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The stalling speed of an helicopter blade.",opts:["is increased when the helicopter is heavier.","is increased when the helicopter is lighter.","does not change."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Helicopters in forward flight are usually.",opts:["directionally stable.","laterally stable.","longitudinally stable."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Stability of a helicopter is its.",opts:["ability to hover at a fixed point above the ground.","ability to move in all 3 axis where and when required.","ability to return to original attitude after displacement."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In forward flight the relative air velocity at each blade.",opts:["is greatest for the retreating blade.","is equal at all blades due to compensation.","is greatest for the advancing blade."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"What forces are acting on the helicopter during descent?.",opts:["Lift only.","Lift and weight.","Weight only."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The ground cushion effect is apparent.",opts:["in the hover only.","in the hover and at low translational flight close to the ground.","on the ground only."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"If the angle of attack is increased on a main rotor blade.",opts:["there is no change in drag.","there is an increase in drag.","there is a reduction in drag."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A helicopter main rotor blade may be tapered from root to tip to.",opts:["equalise lift along the blade.","reduce induced drag.","reduce the blade weight."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A helicopter main rotor blade is twisted from root to tip to.",opts:["permit the blade to straighten under aerodynamic load.","equalise lift along the blade.","give the blade additional strength."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Translational drift is.",opts:["the tendency for the aircraft to drift laterally.","the tendency for the aircraft to turn to port.","the tendency for the aircraft to pitch nose up."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The choice of aerofoil section for a rotor blade is such that.",opts:["it has a stable centre of pressure.","the CofP moves very slowly.","the CofP moves rapidly in response to pitch changes."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When the rotor blade is flapping up, the angle of attack.",opts:["remains the same as the pitch angle.","is less than the pitch angle.","is greater than the pitch angle."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When the rotor blade is flapping down, the pitch angle.",opts:["is less than the angle of attack.","remains the same.","is greater than the angle of attack."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The tropopause exists at about.",opts:["18,000 ft.","30,000 ft.","36,000 ft."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The rotor blades operate at the best Lift/Drag ratio when their.",opts:["angle of attack is 0 degrees.","pitch angles closely approach the stall angle.","angle of attack is about +3 degrees."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The centre of pressure of an aerofoil is that point on the cord line.",opts:["which moves most, with changes in angle of attack, if the section is symmetrical.","at which the highest pressure is said to act.","at which the lift forces resultant is said to act."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The main rotor assembly of a helicopter provides.",opts:["both lift and horizontal thrust.","lift, a component of which is horizontal so thrust is obtained.","lift, but an unbalanced component in the direction of flight moves the aircraft forward."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An advantage of the symmetrical section blades used on helicopters is that.",opts:["the movement of the centre of pressure with changes of the angle of attack is greater than that of a fixed wing.","the centre of pressure moves forward with changes in angle of attack.","the position of the feather axis and the centre of pressure and centre of gravity coincide, providing stability."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Which part of the rotor disc produces the most lift during forward flight?.",opts:["The mid-span portion.","The front portion.","The rear portion."],ans:1,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The aerofoil shape of a main rotor blade is symmetrical in order to make the blade have.",opts:["the highest possible lift co-efficient when hovering.","the best possible autorotative characteristics.","a relatively stable centre of pressure position with changes in angle of attack."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Aspect ratio is the ratio of the.",opts:["disc diameter to the chord.","blade chord to the disc area.","blade span to the chord."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Disc loading is defined as the.",opts:["ratio of gross weight to disc area.","ratio of blade area to disc area.","ratio of gross weight to total blade area."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A helicopter has a main rotor which rotates anticlockwise viewed from above. What happens if a loss of anti-torque device at cruise speed in flight?.",opts:["Nose pitches up slightly + yaw to right.","Nose pitches up slightly + yaw to left.","No appreciable change."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Over pitch causes.",opts:["an increase in RPM.","a reduction in RPM.","blades to cone up."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A shrouded tail rotor.",opts:["reduces need for cyclic feathering.","gives control in pitch and yaw.","has increased airflow so yaw can be controlled by the rudder."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In a helicopter with its main rotor turning anti-clockwise, which way does the aircraft tend to drift?.",opts:["Port if tail rotor is mounted on the right.","Starboard, irrespective of which side the tail rotor is mounted on.","Port if tail rotor is mounted on the left."],ans:1,exp:"08.4. Flight Stability and Dynamics."},
    {topic:"",q:"Dihedral wings combat instability in.",opts:["yaw.","side-slip.","pitch."],ans:1,exp:"As the aircraft side-slips, there is a greater angle of attack on the lower wing which increases lift,straightens the aircraft and stops the side-slip."},
    {topic:"",q:"An aircraft, which is longitudinally stable, will tend to return to level flight after a movement in which axis?.",opts:["Pitch.","Yaw.","Roll."],ans:0,exp:"Longitudinal stability is stability about the pitch axis."},
    {topic:"",q:"The normal axis of an aircraft passes through.",opts:["the centre of gravity.","a point at the centre of the wings.","at the centre of pressure."],ans:0,exp:"All the axis of the aircraft (normal, longitudinal and lateral) pass through the centre of gravity."},
    {topic:"",q:"Due to the change in downwash on an untapered wing (i.e. one of constant chord length) it will.",opts:["not provide any damping effect when rolling.","not suffer adverse yaw effects when turning.","tend to stall first at the root."],ans:2,exp:"The change in downwash is caused by the wingtip vortices, which has a lesser affect inboard than it does at the tip. The downwash reduces the effective angle of attack (more so at the tip). This causes the root of the wing to stall before the tip."},
    {topic:"",q:"Correcting for a disturbance which has caused a rolling motion about the longitudinal axis would re-establish which of the following?.",opts:["Lateral stability.","Longitudinal stability.","Directional stability."],ans:0,exp:"The aircraft's response to rolling is lateral stability."},
    {topic:"",q:"Porpoising is an oscillatory motion in the.",opts:["yaw plane.","roll plane.","pitch plane."],ans:2,exp:"Porpoising is an oscillatory motion in pitch, about the lateral axis."},
    {topic:"",q:"Directional stability is maintained.",opts:["by the tailplane, and controlled by the elevators.","by the keel surface and fin, and controlled by the rudder.","by the mainplanes, and controlled by the ailerons."],ans:1,exp:"Directional stability is maintained by the keel surface and the fin and controlled by the rudder."},
    {topic:"",q:"Longitudinal stability is given by.",opts:["the fin.","the wing dihedral.","the horizontal tailplane."],ans:2,exp:"The horizontal stabilizer (tailplane) provides longitudinal stability."},
    {topic:"",q:"Lateral stability is given by.",opts:["the horizontal tailplane.","the ailerons.","the wing dihedral."],ans:2,exp:"The wing dihedral provides lateral stability."},
    {topic:"",q:"Stability about the lateral axis is given by.",opts:["the ailerons.","the horizontal tailplane.","wing dihedral."],ans:1,exp:"Stability about the lateral axis is longitudinal stability. The horizontal stabilizer (tailplane) provides longitudinal stability."},
    {topic:"",q:"Sweepback of the wings will.",opts:["decrease lateral stability.","not affect the lateral stability.","increase lateral stability."],ans:2,exp:"When an aircraft rolls, it sideslips. A sideslipping aircraft with sweepback has a higher AR on the leading wing than than it has on the trailing wing. The lift is greater on a higher AR wing, so it corrects the roll. (10 degrees of sweepback has the same effect as 1 degree of dihedral)."},
    {topic:"",q:"Dutch Roll is.",opts:["primarily a pitching instability.","a combined rolling and yawing motion.","a type of slow roll."],ans:1,exp:"Dutch role is a combination of role and yaw."},
    {topic:"",q:"A high wing position gives.",opts:["more lateral stability than a low wing.","less lateral stability than a low wing.","the same lateral stability as a low wing."],ans:0,exp:"The pendulum effect produced by the fuselage of a high wing aircraft provides more lateral stability."},
    {topic:"",q:"Directional stability may be increased with.",opts:["pitch dampers.","horn balance.","yaw dampers."],ans:2,exp:"Yaw dampers increase directional stability."},
    {topic:"",q:"Lateral stability may be increased with.",opts:["increased lateral dihedral.","increased lateral anhedral.","increased longitudinal dihedral."],ans:0,exp:"Lateral dihedral increases lateral stability. (BTW: Longitudinal dihedral is the difference between mainplane and tailplane angles of incidence)."},
    {topic:"",q:"Longitudinal stability is increased if the.",opts:["CG is forward of the CP.","Thrust acts on a line below the total drag.","CP moves forward of the CG."],ans:0,exp:"If the CG is forward of the CP it will have a nose-down tendency which makes the aircraft less likely to stall and therefore it will have more longitudinal stability."},
    {topic:"",q:"Directional stability is about the.",opts:["lateral axis.","longitudinal axis.","normal axis."],ans:2,exp:"Directional stability is stability about the normal axis."},
    {topic:"",q:"Lateral stability is about the.",opts:["longitudinal axis.","normal axis.","vertical axis."],ans:0,exp:"Lateral stability is stability about the longitudinal axis."},
    {topic:"",q:"Longitudinal stability is provided by the.",opts:["horizontal stabilizer.","vertical stabilizer.","mainplane."],ans:0,exp:"Longitudinal stability is provided by the horizontal stabiliser (tailplane)."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the aircraft turns and side-slips.",opts:["the sweepback of the wing will correct the sideslip.","the keel surface will correct the sideslip.","the dihedral of the wing will correct the sideslip."],ans:2,exp:"http: //"},
    {topic:"",q:"An aircraft disturbed from its normal flight path, and automatically returns to that normal flight path, without any action on the part of the pilot is known as.",opts:["aircraft stall.","aircraft instability.","aircraft stability."],ans:2,exp:"Stability is the aircraft's ability for the aircraft to return to its normal flightpath after being disturbed."},
    {topic:"",q:"The fin gives stability about which axis?.",opts:["Longitudinal axis.","Lateral axis.","Normal axis."],ans:2,exp:"The fin gives stability about the normal axis."},
    {topic:"",q:"If the nose of the aircraft is rotated about its lateral axis, what is its directional movement?.",opts:["Rolling or banking to the left or right.","Turning to the left or right.","Climbing or diving."],ans:2,exp:"For an aircraft to climb or dive it must be rotated about ts lateral axis."},
    {topic:"",q:"The function of an aircraft fin.",opts:["is to provide directional control.","is to provide straight airflow across the rudder.","is to provide stability about the normal axis."],ans:2,exp:"The function of the aircraft fin is to provide stability about the normal axis."},
    {topic:"",q:"Movement of an aircraft about its normal axis.",opts:["is rolling.","is yawing.","is pitching."],ans:1,exp:"yawing' is movement of the aircraft about its normal axis."},
    {topic:"",q:"If, after a disturbance, an aeroplane initially returns to its equilibrium state.",opts:["it has neutral stability.","it has static stability and may be dynamically stable.","it is neutrally unstable."],ans:1,exp:"Static stability is when an aircraft returns to its equilibrium (trimmed) state. Dynamic stability is the ability of the aircraft to oppose the disturbance."},
    {topic:"",q:"Stability of an aircraft is.",opts:["the tendency of the aircraft to return to its original trimmed position after having been displaced.","the tendency of the aircraft to stall at low airspeed.","the ability of the aircraft to rotate about an axis."],ans:0,exp:"Stability is the tendency for the aircraft to return to its original position after being displaced."},
    {topic:"",q:"The three axes concerned with stability of an aircraft have.",opts:["normal axis through C of G. Lateral axis - wing tip to wing tip. Longitudinal axis - nose to tail but not through C of G.","longitudinal, lateral and normal axis all passing through aircraft centre of gravity.","longitudinal axis nose to tail, lateral axis at furthest span point, normal axis through centre of pressure."],ans:1,exp:"The longitudinal, lateral and normal axis all pass through the aircraft's centre of gravity."},
    {topic:"",q:"If an aircraft returns to a position of equilibrium it is said to be.",opts:["positively stable.","neutrally stable.","negatively stable."],ans:0,exp:"If an aircraft is positively stable it will return to its trimmed position."},
    {topic:"",q:"The pendulum effect on a high wing aircraft.",opts:["has no effect on lateral stability.","increases lateral stability.","decreases lateral stability."],ans:1,exp:"Pendulum effect on a high wing aircraft increases lateral stability."},
    {topic:"",q:"After a disturbance in pitch, an aircraft continues to oscillate at constant amplitude. It is.",opts:["longitudinally unstable.","longitudinally neutrally stable.","laterally unstable."],ans:1,exp:"If an aircraft oscillates in pitch without the oscillations increasing or decreasing it is longitudinally neutrally stable."},
    {topic:"",q:"On an aircraft with an all-moving tailplane nose up pitch is caused by.",opts:["decreasing tailplane incidence.","increasing tailplane incidence.","up movement of the trim tab."],ans:0,exp:"To make the nose pitch-up, the tailplane down load must be increased. This is done by decreasing its incidence (or increasing its negative incidence)."},
    {topic:"",q:"What gives the aircraft directional stability?.",opts:["Vertical stabiliser.","Elevators.","Horizontal stabiliser."],ans:0,exp:"The vertical stabiliser gives the aircraft directional stability."},
    {topic:"",q:"In flight if the aircraft nose gets an upward gust of wind, what characteristic will have the greatest effect to counteract it?.",opts:["Wing Sweep.","Horizontal stabiliser and fuselage length.","Position of the centre of pressure relative to the centre of gravity."],ans:1,exp:"Longitudinal stability is effected mainly by the stabiliser and length of fuselage behind the C of G."},
    {topic:"",q:"To correct dutch roll you must damp oscillation around:.",opts:["the longitudinal axis.","the lateral axis.","the vertical axis."],ans:2,exp:"Dutch Roll is a figure of eight oscillation around the vertical axis."},
    {topic:"",q:"An elevator trim tab is used to.",opts:["counteract propeller torque.","prevent the control surface from stalling the airflow.","reduce control column forces on the pilot."],ans:2,exp:"See AC Kermode Chapter 9."},
    {topic:"",q:"A high wing aircraft will be more.",opts:["laterally stable than a low wing aircraft.","longitudinally stable than a low wing aircraft.","directionally stable than a low wing aircraft."],ans:0,exp:"Due to pendulum effect of the fuselage, a high wing aircraft will be more laterally stable than a low wing aircraft."},
    {topic:"",q:"After an aircraft has been disturbed from its straight and level flight, it returns to its original attitude with a small amount of decreasing oscillation.The aircraft is.",opts:["statically stable but dynamically unstable.","statically unstable but dynamically stable.","statically stable and dynamically stable."],ans:2,exp:"Static stability is the ability of the aircraft to return to its untrimmed position. Dynamic stability is the ability of the aircraft to not oscillate about the trimmed position."},
    {topic:"",q:"If there is an increase of density, what effect would there be in aerodynamic dampening?.",opts:["Decreased.","Increased.","None."],ans:1,exp:"Flight forces (and hence aerodynamic dampening) are all increased with increasing density."},
    {topic:"",q:"Yawing is a rotation around.",opts:["the lateral axis obtained by the rudder.","the normal axis obtained by the rudder.","the normal axis obtained by the elevator."],ans:1,exp:"Yawing is rotation around the normal axis obtained by the rudder."},
    {topic:"",q:"Lateral stability is reduced by increasing.",opts:["dihedral.","sweepback.","anhedral."],ans:2,exp:"Anhedral is a downward and outward inclination of the wing. It is opposite to dihedral."},
    {topic:"",q:"Azimuth stability is dependent on.",opts:["dihedral.","keel and fin.","tailplane."],ans:1,exp:"Azimuth is a fancy word for 'direction'."},
    {topic:"",q:"Sweepback of the wings will.",opts:["increase lateral stability at high speeds only.","not affect lateral stability.","increase lateral stability at all speeds."],ans:2,exp:"10 degrees of sweepback provides the same effect as 1 degree of dihedral."},
    {topic:"",q:"If you have an aircraft that is more laterally stable then directionally stable it will tend to:.",opts:["bank.","slip.","skid."],ans:2,exp:"Skidding out of turns is symptomatic of dutch roll, extra vertical stabilisers are often added to correct this (Nimrod MR2)."},
    {topic:"",q:"A centre of gravity position close to its aft limit will cause the aircraft to.",opts:["pitch nose down and increase its longitudinal stability.","pitch nose up and decrease its longitudinal stability.","pitch nose up and increase its longitudinal stability."],ans:1,exp:"AC Kermode page 145 states that a pitch up will increase AOA and further destabilise the aircraft."},
    {topic:"",q:"A sharply swept wing will promote.",opts:["excessive lateral instability.","excessive longitudinal stability.","excessive lateral stability."],ans:2,exp:"Swept wing fighter aircraft often have anhedral to promote greater roll rate, which is needed due to excessive stability."},
    {topic:"",q:"Which control surfaces provide lateral control, also longitudinal control and stability?.",opts:["Ruddervators.","Tailerons.","Flapperons."],ans:1,exp:"Tailerons are all moving tailplanes as fitted to the Tornado\"."},
    {topic:"",q:"If, after a disturbance, an aeroplane initially returns to its equilibrium state.",opts:["it has neutral stability.","it has static stability and may be dynamically stable.","it is neutrally unstable."],ans:1,exp:"Question says nothing about overshooting and oscillating, so it could be dynamically unstable, even though it is statically stable."},
    {topic:"",q:"Yaw dampers are designed to.",opts:["prevent dutch roll.","assist the pilot to move the rudder.","reduce the effect of crabbing due to cross winds."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"Tuck under occurs when.",opts:["a shock stall occurs on the outboard portion of swept wing.","a shock stall warning occurs on the inboard position of a straight wing.","the aircraft reaches Mcrit."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"The lateral axis is.",opts:["a straight line through the CG at right angles to the longitudinal and lateral axis.","a straight line through the CG from nose to tail.","a straight line through the CG parallel to a line joining the wingtips."],ans:2,exp:"A straight line through the CG parallel to a line joining the wingtip."},
    {topic:"",q:"The main factors which affect longitudinal stability are.",opts:["design of the fuselage and position of the CG.","design of the mainplane and position of the CG.","design of the tailplane and position of the CG."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"A yawing motion provides what kind of Stability?.",opts:["Directional.","Lateral.","Longitudinal."],ans:0,exp:"Yawing is directional stability."},
    {topic:"",q:"Where would you find the normal axis?.",opts:["Through C of G at right angles to longitudinal and lateral axis.","Vertically through CofP.","In line with the wing tips through C of G."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"When a aircraft is in a slideslip and is yawing the.",opts:["the fin will correct the yawing motion.","the effective keel area will make the ac yaw further into the direction of the sideslip.","the dihedral will prevent the yaw motion."],ans:1,exp:"Page 290 Mechanics of Flight Kermode."},
    {topic:"",q:"As a consequence of the C of G being close to its aft limit.",opts:["the stick forces to manoeuvre longitudinally will be low due to low stability.","the stick forces will be high in fore and aft pitch, due to the high longitudinal stability.","the stick forces when pitching the nose down will be very high."],ans:2,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"With the C of G on its forward limit.",opts:["the change in control loading is dependant on the position of the CofP.","control loading decreases.","control loading increases."],ans:2,exp:"08.5."},
    {topic:"",q:"A 'slug' is a unit of.",opts:["mass.","density.","volume."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"An undercarriage leg in flight produces 3 lbs of drag at 100kts. If speed is increased to 200kts the drag would be.",opts:["12 lbs.","9 lbs.","6 lbs."],ans:0,exp:"Profile drag rises with V squared."},
    {topic:"",q:"A stall warning device must be set to operate.",opts:["at a speed just above stalling speed.","at a speed just below stalling speed.","at the stalling speed."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"In cruise the weight of an aeroplane is decreasing as fuel is used. A stall would occur.",opts:["at a lower speed.","at the same speed.","at a higher speed."],ans:0,exp:"Stall speed increases with increasing weight."},
    {topic:"",q:"The purpose of 'streamlining' is to reduce.",opts:["profile drag.","skin friction drag.","induced drag."],ans:0,exp:""},
    {topic:"",q:"As height increases, with angle of attack and speed constant.",opts:["lift will remain constant.","lift Increases.","lift decreases."],ans:2,exp:"That is why a greater angle of attack is required at higher altitude."}
